A||N||5||87fastback||87 5-speed shifting problems??||||1||
Z||000000||87fastback||11-07-2001||12:13 AM||||This is my 2nd Fiero, my first was an 85 GT 4-speed and it shifted very smoothly.  The trouble I am having is it is very hard to get into first and second gear.  It feels very notchy and it takes pushing the clutch pedal to the floor to get to even go into gear, even when the car is not running and the thing is sitting on level ground with the e-brake on.  It also grinds VERY badly when trying to put it into third gear no matter if you are driving or just sitting.  Also takes ALOT of effort to put it into reverse.<P>I have replaced the master cylinder and rebuilt the slave and to no avail, it acts the same way.  As a matter of fact I ended up going back to the original master cylinder as it worked better than the new one.  I have bleed the system a hundred times using Archie's method and many others, still no go.  There are no leaks as I have removed both the master and slave and checked the boots and they were dry of fluid.<P>I have tried adjusting the cables and it still is VERY difficult to shift.  It is a MG2 tranny, not the Isuzu unit.  The shift lever is the steel unit and the clutch pedal looks to be the steel one.  It looks like it is moving the slave rod as soon as you start pushing the pedal.  The clutch does not slip and I am confident the actual disc and cover are in good condition.<P>Any suggestions would be helpful.<P>ebay4now@yahoo.com||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rich86GT||11-07-2001||11:11 PM||||I had a similar problem with my 5 speed. It started grinding going into reverse and first, then second, and it just kept getting worse. When I started the car, clutch fully pushed, it would move the car a litte like the clutch wasn't released. I bled the clutch, rebuilt cylinders, bled the clutch, checked the pedal, bled the clutch, checked the pedal bushings, bled the clutch, measured the travel of the master cylinder (1.2") when pushing the pedal down, measured the travel of the slave cylinder (0.9"). The reason for the difference in travel is the diameter of the slave cylinder is bigger than the master. One day while forcing it into 5th, the transmission went into neutral and I was unable to get it into gear again.  After having the transmission rebuilt (bent fork in the transmission), I put in a new clutch.  I should have done that sooner, would have save a $x,xxx on the transmission.  The old clutch had 35,000 on it. The clutch works great now and releases just fine.<P>Good luck.||||reg||12
Z||000002||87fastback||11-07-2001||11:17 PM||||Well I have since rebuilt both the master and slave - again . . . it has gotten much better.  I rebuilt the original master by the way which worked MUCH better then the NEW aftermarket Wagner unit.  I bleed the system a bunch last night and it seemed much better, though the test is always it sitting over night.  Was good this morning as well, though it still is kind of notchy.<P>I have been reading alot that changing the fluid in the transmission makes a big difference going to the synchro-mesh GM stuff from the ATF that is in it now.  I am going to try that as well tomorrow.<P>Lubed the shift cables and all pivots and that seemed to help a little as well.  Hope I don't have to replace the clutch like you did, but I just don't see that it could be the clutch itself since the problem will come and go with different master/cylinder combos and bleeding.<P>Thanks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||SLYGUY||11-08-2001||03:48 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87fastback:<BR><B>Well I have since rebuilt both the master and slave - again . . . it has gotten much better.  I rebuilt the original master by the way which worked MUCH better then the NEW aftermarket Wagner unit.  I bleed the system a bunch last night and it seemed much better, though the test is always it sitting over night.  Was good this morning as well, though it still is kind of notchy.<P>I have been reading alot that changing the fluid in the transmission makes a big difference going to the synchro-mesh GM stuff from the ATF that is in it now.  I am going to try that as well tomorrow.<P>Lubed the shift cables and all pivots and that seemed to help a little as well.  Hope I don't have to replace the clutch like you did, but I just don't see that it could be the clutch itself since the problem will come and go with different master/cylinder combos and bleeding.<P>Thanks.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I was having the same problems and it turned out to be the master cylinder. I didn't bother with all of the work/time involved with rebuilding it. I bought a new GM unit for $111 at the dealer. Bolted it in, filled it and bled it for about 5 mins. with a mityvac and it works great. <BR>Only problem I have had is that I stopped bleeding the system the moment the clutch felt good and a month later the clutch was disengaging at the floor and then not at all. 5 mins with the mityvac fixed all that. I'm gonna use that bleeder everyday till all that freakin' air is gone. So much air!!!<P>My 5spd in my 88GT shifts well. It can be a little notchy if you rush it but besides that it is great.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||87fastback||11-11-2001||12:48 PM||||I ended up taking another members advice and change my Getrag fluid from the ATF it had in it to the GM synchromesh.  Boy what a difference just fluid makes!  Though it is still not 100% it is much smoother to shift.  It is pretty good going in and out of all gears but is a little notchy going into first still, and always grinds when shifting from second to third if you don't let the engine wind down for a few seconds before putting it into third.  I think that part of the problem might be the selector arm on the transmission.  The pivot pin is kind of sloppy and if it doesn't move the arm the entire distance needed it is notchy into first every time.  I pulled both cables off the arms and shifted it manually and it shifted nicely when I pushed the selector all the way and then to first.  I am going to get the pivot rebuild kit from Rodney this week!  And then to fix my sagging doors!||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||11-11-2001||08:23 PM||||Make sure the slave cylinder is push rod end down. If its pointing up hill you'll have the same kind of problem bleeding it as the MC problem in my brake service article.<P>GM recomends only Syncromesh fluid for the Muncie 5 speed transmissions. Various notes about that are in my cave.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
